Splitting Text into Chunks--

Given an input text (a long string) and a specified maximum chunk size (an integer), the task is to split the text into chunks such that each chunk is at most the provided size. The chunks should, as much as possible, avoid splitting words in half. If a single word exceeds the maximum chunk size, it may be split across multiple chunks.

Constraints:

The input is a non-empty string (though it can also be empty; see edge cases).
The maximum chunk size is a positive integer.
Words are separated by whitespace.
Ideally, words should remain intact in a chunk unless they themselves exceed the maximum chunk size.
Section 2 — Intuition
The core insight is to iterate over the words in the text and build chunks by appending words until adding the next word would exceed the maximum size. If it would, start a new chunk. This greedy approach is simple and effective: by processing words sequentially, it preserves the logical structure of the text as chunks without breaking words arbitrarily.

Section 3 — Approach 1: Brute Force
A naive method is to consider every possible substring split and check if it satisfies the conditions (i.e., not splitting words if possible). This approach, however, would generate all possible segments and select the one that yields the best outcome.

Time Complexity:
O(n²) in the worst-case scenario, because checking every possible substring split is inefficient.

Space Complexity:
O(n), for storing potential substrings.

Below is a simplified implementation in JavaScript of a brute force approach (note that this method is not recommended for production due to inefficiency):

Copy
js
/**
 * Brute force method to split text into chunks.
 * This method is not optimal because it checks many possible splits.
 *
 * @param {string} text - The input text.
 * @param {number} maxChunkSize - Maximum allowed chunk length.
 * @returns {string[]} - Array of text chunks.
 */
function splitTextBruteForce(text, maxChunkSize) {
    // Base case: if text length is 0 or less than or equal to maxChunkSize, return as a single chunk.
    if (!text || text.length <= maxChunkSize) return [text];

    let bestChunks = [];
    
    /**
     * Recursive helper that tracks the current index and current chunk list.
     */
    function helper(start, chunks) {
        if (start >= text.length) {
            // Check if current solution is feasible (works at word boundaries)
            if (chunks.join(" ").length >= text.replace/+/g, " ".trim().length) {
                bestChunks = chunks.slice();
            }
            return;
        }
        // Try every possible ending position
        for (let end = start + 1; end <= text.length; end++) {
            const substring = text.substring(start, end);
            if (substring.length > maxChunkSize) break;
            // Try splitting only if the substring ends at a space or at the text end
            if (end === text.length || text[end] === " ") {
                chunks.push(substring.trim());
                helper(end + 1, chunks);
                chunks.pop();
            }
        }
    }
    
    helper(0, []);
    return bestChunks;
}

// Example usage:
const textExample = "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog";
console.log(splitTextBruteForce(textExample, 10));
Time Complexity: O(n²) in worst-case due to nested substring enumeration.
Space Complexity: O(n) for recursion and storing chunks.

Section 4 — Approach 2: Optimal Solution
A more optimal approach is to iterate over words, adding them to the current chunk until appending the next word would cause the chunk’s length to exceed the maximum limit. If a single word is longer than the limit, then that word is split into segments.

Step-by-Step Algorithm:

Split the text using whitespace into an array of words.
Initialize an empty list to hold chunks and an empty string for the current chunk.
For each word in the list:
If adding the word (with a preceding space if needed) does not exceed the max limit, append it.
Otherwise:
Push the current chunk to the result list.
If the word itself exceeds the max chunk size, split the word into pieces of size maxChunkSize.
Otherwise, set the current chunk to the word.
After the loop, push any remaining current chunk to the result list.
Return the list of chunks.
Time Complexity:
O(n), where n is the number of words (each word is examined and processed once).

Space Complexity:
O(n), for the result array containing the chunks.

Below is the optimal solution implementation in JavaScript:

Copy
js
/**
 * Optimal method to split text into chunks without breaking words if possible.
 *
 * @param {string} text - The input text.
 * @param {number} maxChunkSize - Maximum allowed chunk length.
 * @returns {string[]} - Array of text chunks.
 */
function splitTextOptimal(text, maxChunkSize) {
    if (!text) return [];
    
    const words = text.split/+/;
    const chunks = [];
    let currentChunk = "";

    for (let word of words) {
        // Handle case where word itself is longer than allowed chunk size
        if (word.length > maxChunkSize) {
            // If currentChunk has some content, push it first.
            if (currentChunk.length > 0) {
                chunks.push(currentChunk.trim());
                currentChunk = "";
            }
            // Split the overly long word into allowed sizes.
            let index = 0;
            while (index < word.length) {
                const part = word.substring(index, index + maxChunkSize);
                chunks.push(part);
                index += maxChunkSize;
            }
        } else {
            // Check if adding the next word exceeds the chunk limit.
            if ((currentChunk + " " + word).trim().length <= maxChunkSize) {
                currentChunk = (currentChunk + " " + word).trim();
            } else {
                // Push the current chunk and start a new one with the current word.
                if (currentChunk.length > 0) {
                    chunks.push(currentChunk);
                }
                currentChunk = word;
            }
        }
    }

    // Push the last chunk if exists.
    if (currentChunk.length > 0) {
        chunks.push(currentChunk);
    }
    
    return chunks;
}

// Example usage:
const text = "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog";
const maxSize = 10;
console.log(splitTextOptimal(text, maxSize));
Time Complexity: O(n), since each word is processed once and splitting a word over the chunk size is proportional to the word's length.
Space Complexity: O(n), for storing and returning the chunks.

Section 5 — Approach Comparison Table
Approach	Time Complexity	Space Complexity	Recommended
Brute Force	O(n²)	O(n)	No
Optimal	O(n)	O(n)	Yes
Section 6 — Detailed Dry Run
Consider the text: "The quick brown fox" with maxChunkSize = 10.

Step	word	currentChunk (before adding)	Condition Check	Action	Chunks after step
1	"The"	""	("" + "The") length 3 <= 10	currentChunk becomes "The"	[]
2	"quick"	"The"	("The quick") length 9 <= 10	currentChunk becomes "The quick"	[]
3	"brown"	"The quick"	("The quick brown") length 15 > 10	Push "The quick" to chunks; currentChunk becomes "brown"	["The quick"]
4	"fox"	"brown"	("brown fox") length 9 <= 10	currentChunk becomes "brown fox"	["The quick"]
End	-	"brown fox"	End of iteration	Push "brown fox" to chunks	["The quick", "brown fox"]
Section 7 — Edge Cases
Empty text:
Input: "" → Expected output: [].

All same elements / repeated words:
Input: "test test test", maxChunkSize = 5 → Expected: Each "test" becomes its own chunk if combined exceeds limit.

Text with a single extremely long word:
Input: "supercalifragilisticexpialidocious", maxChunkSize = 10 → Expected: The word is split into segments like ["supercalif", "ragilistic", "expialidoc", "ious"].

Text with two words exactly fitting the chunk size:
Input: "hello world", maxChunkSize = 11 → Expected: ["hello world"].

Text that is already less than or equal to chunk size:
Input: "hello", maxChunkSize = 10 → Expected: ["hello"].

Section 8 — Common Mistakes
Not trimming the current chunk before checking its length, leading to off-by-one issues due to extra spaces.
• Fix: Always trim the chunk before checking its length.

Forgetting to handle words that individually exceed the maximum chunk size, which may result in an infinite loop or dropped text.
• Fix: Explicitly check and split words longer than the max chunk size.

Not adding the last chunk after finishing iteration.
• Fix: After loop completion, if the current chunk is non-empty, push it into the result list.

Using a simple string split with a fixed interval without considering word boundaries.
• Fix: Process words individually to preserve natural word boundaries.

Overcomplicating the approach with recursion when a simple iterative method suffices.
• Fix: Use an efficient iterative method with clear if-else conditions.

Section 9 — Interview Tips
Explain that the problem tests your ability to perform string manipulation and greedy algorithms.
Mention that handling edge cases, especially when a word exceeds the maximum length, is crucial.
State that you would first split the text by whitespace and then intelligently aggregate words to form chunks.
Expect follow-up questions such as: • "How would you modify the solution to also preserve punctuation?" • "Can you optimize this if the text is extremely long (streaming data)?" • "What if the text contains multiple types of whitespace or newline characters?"
